1531
00:02:40,529 --> 00:02:42,749
Oh my goodness...

1532
00:02:42,749 --> 00:02:44,929
Are you done getting ready, Eli?

1533
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:47,409
Yes.

1534
00:02:54,399 --> 00:02:57,299
Looks like your natural aura is stronger than the clothes...

1535
00:02:57,299 --> 00:03:01,139
You are clearly a noble lady trying to disguise yourself.

1536
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:03,139
That's not good.

1537
00:03:03,139 --> 00:03:07,979
<i>Your Highness, all of that applies to you as well.</i>

1538
00:03:11,279 --> 00:03:15,409
<i>I've felt antsy ever since that incident the other day.</i>

1539
00:03:17,489 --> 00:03:22,119
<i>Like even though I want to be with the prince,
I want to run away...</i>

1540
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:27,249
<i>but if I'm away from him I feel
so lonely I can hardly stand it.</i>

1541
00:03:27,589 --> 00:03:29,879
If it's so bad, then call it off.

1542
00:03:29,879 --> 00:03:36,339
Glen, you should save whatever else you have to say,
or I'll make you listen to Alec's lecture all by yourself.

1543
00:03:36,339 --> 00:03:38,589
Don't make horrible threats so off-handedly...

1544
00:03:38,589 --> 00:03:40,009
Your Highness...

1545
00:03:40,009 --> 00:03:42,139
Why are you making yourself go out?

1546
00:03:43,409 --> 00:03:44,519
The Stars of Thistle.

1547
00:03:45,849 --> 00:03:51,319
You told me you wanted to go and see and choose
from their selection of books at least once, didn't you, Eli?

1548
00:03:52,399 --> 00:03:56,069
If we miss it today, we'll have to wait three years
for it to come again.

1549
00:03:56,819 --> 00:03:57,959
Butâ¦

1550
00:03:59,989 --> 00:04:06,869
Today, I am merely your knight.
Lady Elianna, I will protect you.

1551
00:04:07,389 --> 00:04:09,879
Will you venture out with me today?

1552
00:04:13,319 --> 00:04:19,139
<i>The Stars of Thistle is a troupeof scholars
famous throughout this land. </i><i></i>

1553
00:04:19,649 --> 00:04:24,139
<i>Without putting down roots in any specific country,
they travel with their compatriots,</i>

1554
00:04:24,519 --> 00:04:28,139
<i>and I hear there are those that avoid them
as they are a nomadic people.</i>

1555
00:04:29,059 --> 00:04:33,899
<i>Since I heard that once every three years
they open up a book market</i>

1556
00:04:34,309 --> 00:04:38,909
<i>with a generous display of newly published
and rare and foreign books,</i>

1557
00:04:38,909 --> 00:04:44,259
<i>it's been a place I've longed to go for a very long time.</i>

1558
00:04:45,229 --> 00:04:53,129
<i>His Highness knew of my wish, most likely,
from a conversation we had when we were children.</i>

1559
00:04:53,719 --> 00:04:58,129
<i>But, why can't remember it?</i>

1560
00:04:58,129 --> 00:04:59,879
What's the matter, Eli?

1561
00:04:59,879 --> 00:05:02,719
Nothing, Your Highness, umâ¦

1562
00:05:02,719 --> 00:05:05,639
Today, you are forbidden
from calling me "Your Highness."

1563
00:05:05,639 --> 00:05:09,519
Which means, I want you to call me by my name.

1564
00:05:09,519 --> 00:05:10,659
Huh?

1565
00:05:10,659 --> 00:05:12,519
Shall we practice, Eli?

1566
00:05:12,519 --> 00:05:15,529
Y-Yes, Your Highness.

1567
00:05:17,669 --> 00:05:21,529
How about if you get punished
every time you call me "Your Highness"?

1568
00:05:21,929 --> 00:05:24,659
Of course, I get to decide on the punishment.

1569
00:05:46,389 --> 00:05:47,639
Eli?

1570
00:05:47,639 --> 00:05:51,809
Make sure you keep your hand in mine all day today.

1571
00:05:52,119 --> 00:05:54,189
Okay, Christopher!

1572
00:05:59,619 --> 00:06:00,419
Excuse me, sir...

1573
00:06:00,419 --> 00:06:04,069
Do you have Dr. Rezzi's "Writings of the Land"
that was released three years ago?

1574
00:06:04,409 --> 00:06:08,449
The writings of Dr. Rezzi are not only famous,
but there are very few in circulation.

1575
00:06:08,449 --> 00:06:10,409
They sell out very quickly.

1576
00:06:10,409 --> 00:06:12,369
Same as always, huh?

1577
00:06:14,119 --> 00:06:15,419
"The Star Traveler"?

1578
00:06:15,869 --> 00:06:18,919
Those are fairy tales for children,
but adults can enjoy them too.

1579
00:06:18,919 --> 00:06:22,069
This is the Sulu Qwun language version.

1580
00:06:22,069 --> 00:06:22,939
Huh?

1581
00:06:22,939 --> 00:06:29,559
If possible, I'd like one written in old Lacan,
or one written during old Kai Arg Empire,

1582
00:06:29,899 --> 00:06:33,639
but I'm most interested
in the Fire Thorns version if possible.

1583
00:06:33,639 --> 00:06:34,439
What?

1584
00:06:34,439 --> 00:06:38,019
Sir, do you have by any chance have "The Star Traveler"?

1585
00:06:38,019 --> 00:06:42,029
I'd like any version other than one written
in the Sulu Qwun language if possible.

1586
00:06:42,029 --> 00:06:45,449
O-Okay... Give me a moment.

1587
00:06:46,909 --> 00:06:50,449
Not everyone is so well-versed in books.

1588
00:06:50,449 --> 00:06:52,999
I suppose not, Your Hiâ

1589
00:06:52,999 --> 00:06:55,329
Lord Chris. That's quite insightful of you.

1590
00:06:56,639 --> 00:07:02,839
Perhaps. But I wasn't aware that "The Star Traveler"
had been written in so many different languages.

1591
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:08,009
The story is also a little different depending
on the time period and the country in which it was written.

1592
00:07:08,009 --> 00:07:11,599
It was a book my mother liked,
so my father collected them.

1593
00:07:11,599 --> 00:07:16,109
That's interesting. Maybe I'll give it another read as well.

1594
00:07:16,109 --> 00:07:18,859
Do you read fairy tales as well, Your Highness?

1595
00:07:18,859 --> 00:07:19,859
Eli.

1596
00:07:21,109 --> 00:07:24,419
Do you want me to punish you that badly?

1597
00:07:24,419 --> 00:07:28,119
You two... can you do your flirting somewhere else?

1598
00:07:32,929 --> 00:07:35,369
Good thing he had the old Lacan version, right?

1599
00:07:35,749 --> 00:07:41,919
Yes. Oh, this is the book
that Lord Theodore was searching for.

1600
00:07:43,319 --> 00:07:47,929
My uncle has both the time and the money.
He can come purchase things himself.

1601
00:07:47,929 --> 00:07:49,389
Yes, but...

1602
00:07:49,389 --> 00:07:52,009
You are also forbidden from saying another man's name.

1603
00:07:53,069 --> 00:07:53,889
Your....

1604
00:07:55,449 --> 00:07:57,349
O'er yonder lies a farm...

1605
00:08:01,469 --> 00:08:03,189
Why are you here?

1606
00:08:03,699 --> 00:08:08,199
Surely you don't expect me
to miss this entertaining exhibition?

1607
00:08:09,739 --> 00:08:11,469
Is this someone you know?

1608
00:08:13,169 --> 00:08:13,909
Nope.

1609
00:08:15,149 --> 00:08:17,959
Seems he's a stranger. Let's go.

1610
00:08:17,959 --> 00:08:19,919
H-Hey...

1611
00:08:21,129 --> 00:08:22,829
Thief!

1612
00:08:26,669 --> 00:08:28,759
Wait! Stop him!

1613
00:08:31,729 --> 00:08:33,009
Got him!

1614
00:08:33,009 --> 00:08:34,429
Lemme go!

1615
00:08:37,389 --> 00:08:40,229
You brats have the nerve to steal
from a store run by your own people?!

1616
00:08:40,229 --> 00:08:41,939
Kids can't sell books on their own!

1617
00:08:42,459 --> 00:08:45,439
No! I was told to go steal this!

1618
00:08:45,439 --> 00:08:49,319
What? Who in the world would say such a thing?!

1619
00:08:49,319 --> 00:08:51,319
One of the Bernstein nobles said it!

1620
00:08:54,129 --> 00:08:55,349
Bernstein?

1621
00:08:55,349 --> 00:08:56,449
And where is this noble?!

1622
00:09:01,499 --> 00:09:02,719
Lord Chris?

1623
00:09:03,839 --> 00:09:06,539
Where is that noble?!

1624
00:09:06,539 --> 00:09:07,839
May I have a word?

1625
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:08,719
Huh?

1626
00:09:14,089 --> 00:09:16,219
What do you want?

1627
00:09:16,549 --> 00:09:18,729
Do you know these brats?

1628
00:09:19,239 --> 00:09:20,599
Dad!

1629
00:09:25,789 --> 00:09:28,009
You're these scamps father, then?

1630
00:09:39,659 --> 00:09:41,539
And so...

1631
00:09:42,069 --> 00:09:45,039
I will take custody of these two.

1632
00:09:46,519 --> 00:09:48,089
The gist of it is:

1633
00:09:48,089 --> 00:09:51,759
The mother of the child named Rene is sick
and wants to see a doctor,

1634
00:09:51,759 --> 00:09:54,539
but the wandering Thistle people will not see her.

1635
00:09:54,539 --> 00:09:55,009
Yeah.

1636
00:09:55,009 --> 00:10:00,229
Knowing that, a noble man who approached him said
in exchange for stealing a book from the market,

1637
00:10:00,229 --> 00:10:02,519
he promised to have a doctor see his mother.

1638
00:10:02,519 --> 00:10:03,269
Yeah.

1639
00:10:03,719 --> 00:10:06,769
And you have decided to give this child a helping hand.

1640
00:10:07,049 --> 00:10:09,529
Rene is like a little brother to me, you know.

1641
00:10:09,549 --> 00:10:16,239
I understood that much, but why does
Rene believe that Chris is his father?

1642
00:10:16,239 --> 00:10:19,909
What's there to believe? That guy really is his dad, right?

1643
00:10:22,789 --> 00:10:27,099
Eli, I don't have anything to hide, okay?

1644
00:10:28,339 --> 00:10:30,919
My dad is a knight.

1645
00:10:31,329 --> 00:10:37,809
He has the same blue eyes as me,
and my mom said he'd for sure come and meet me here.

1646
00:10:39,349 --> 00:10:44,339
Ah, so basically, based solely on
the characteristics told to you by your mother,

1647
00:10:44,339 --> 00:10:47,179
both you and that child have determined
that Chris is his father.

1648
00:10:47,179 --> 00:10:48,899
It's not only that, though.

1649
00:10:48,899 --> 00:10:53,819
Earlier he mentioned the name of the doctor of the Stars of Thistle,
and that made everyone back off, right?

1650
00:10:54,639 --> 00:10:57,579
I'm an old acquaintance of Dr. Rezzi, you know.

1651
00:10:58,089 --> 00:11:00,039
You know the doctor, right?

1652
00:11:01,009 --> 00:11:04,339
Rene's mom was a dancer for the Stars of Thistle,

1653
00:11:04,339 --> 00:11:08,709
but the knight of Sauslind got to know the doctor
because she was his lover.

1654
00:11:08,949 --> 00:11:10,959
Oh, is that right?

1655
00:11:11,309 --> 00:11:13,719
Eli... what's with that look?

1656
00:11:14,149 --> 00:11:15,809
Oh, it's nothing.

1657
00:11:16,529 --> 00:11:22,979
I didn't know you were in that romance novel
about the dancing girl and the knight, Lord Chris.

1658
00:11:24,319 --> 00:11:26,189
Lady Elianna, aren't you hungry?

1659
00:11:26,619 --> 00:11:29,439
Or are you wary of food you've never had before?

1660
00:11:29,769 --> 00:11:30,959
No.

1661
00:11:30,959 --> 00:11:33,149
Everytime I read about foods of eastern countries in

1662
00:11:33,149 --> 00:11:37,699
"The Travels of Parco Molo"
our cook would make them for me.

1663
00:11:38,199 --> 00:11:42,579
The cook for the Bernstein family
must be amazing at all sorts of cuisine.

1664
00:11:43,749 --> 00:11:47,079
Do you happen to know of my family?

1665
00:11:47,629 --> 00:11:49,749
She really doesn't recognize me...

1666
00:11:49,749 --> 00:11:54,089
As someone who may not have less of
a presence than realized... I am Alan.

1667
00:11:53,129 --> 00:11:54,009
I am Alan...

1668
00:11:53,129 --> 00:11:54,009
I am Alan...

1669
00:11:54,529 --> 00:11:57,839
Alan, will you ask for a report from over there? Glen.

1670
00:11:59,449 --> 00:12:02,989
Eli. I'm sorry, go ahead and eat.

1671
00:12:02,989 --> 00:12:03,719
Okay.

1672
00:12:04,229 --> 00:12:05,099
That goes for you too.

1673
00:12:05,589 --> 00:12:09,669
You can't eat if you're facing me. Come on.

1674
00:12:17,579 --> 00:12:20,659
Rene, don't worry about it and eat your food.

1675
00:12:20,659 --> 00:12:21,629
Kay.

1676
00:12:24,329 --> 00:12:28,939
Tiny lady, are you one of the Bernstein nobles?

1677
00:12:28,939 --> 00:12:32,339
Yes. I'm Elianna Bernstein.

1678
00:12:33,019 --> 00:12:35,459
Aren't you relatives of moles?

1679
00:12:36,509 --> 00:12:37,719
Moles?

1680
00:12:38,009 --> 00:12:39,719
The mole nobles.

1681
00:12:39,719 --> 00:12:44,969
Like you sit around all puffed up and cocky.
 I know all about how you look down on us.

1682
00:12:45,269 --> 00:12:51,609
But he said he'd get a doctor to see Rene's mom, 
so I'm being good and going along with him.

1683
00:12:51,609 --> 00:12:53,399
Sorry, Paolo...

1684
00:12:53,399 --> 00:12:54,859
It's not your fault, Rene.

1685
00:12:55,409 --> 00:13:01,119
If there was a doctor that would come see us,
no one would care at all what that guy said!

1686
00:13:02,269 --> 00:13:04,859
<i>A doctor that will see anyone...</i>

1687
00:13:04,859 --> 00:13:07,969
<i>I think I've read a story like that before...</i>

1688
00:13:08,669 --> 00:13:15,129
That's why I absolutely hate you nobles
that act all cocky and take advantage of us!

1689
00:13:15,419 --> 00:13:17,749
Nobles aren't even all that great!

1690
00:13:18,739 --> 00:13:19,879
I don't think...

1691
00:13:20,339 --> 00:13:22,889
All nobles are the same!

1692
00:13:30,239 --> 00:13:33,399
I don't know if you'll get this, but...

1693
00:13:33,779 --> 00:13:39,399
This is a book I read countless times
when I was a child, called "The Star Traveler."

1694
00:13:39,999 --> 00:13:45,989
In the story, the traveler who was treated cruelly
by a crow they encountered in a wasteland said this in return:

1695
00:13:47,259 --> 00:13:51,779
"I'm very sad that you're mistreating me right now."

1696
00:13:51,779 --> 00:13:57,369
"But, when I meet another crow, I will not mistreat it."

1697
00:13:59,529 --> 00:14:03,429
That is the kind of person I want to be.

1698
00:14:05,119 --> 00:14:06,309
Who cares?!

1699
00:14:07,679 --> 00:14:10,429
You can say that all you want without doing a thing!

1700
00:14:12,559 --> 00:14:14,189
That's true.

1701
00:14:19,279 --> 00:14:20,709
Paolo...

1702
00:14:27,399 --> 00:14:30,579
But... what should I do?

1703
00:14:30,579 --> 00:14:31,829
Sorry...

1704
00:14:32,819 --> 00:14:36,589
That was too much. This isn't your fault.

1705
00:14:37,049 --> 00:14:40,839
When I heard you were a Bernstein, I just...

1706
00:14:41,459 --> 00:14:42,399
Sorry!

1707
00:14:49,129 --> 00:14:50,239
Lord Paolo.

1708
00:14:51,209 --> 00:14:52,979
Lord... Paolo?

1709
00:14:53,309 --> 00:14:58,119
The people of my family would never steal a book.

1710
00:14:58,119 --> 00:15:02,609
If it's a book we simply must have and we can't get it...

1711
00:15:03,219 --> 00:15:04,489
"...can't get it"?

1712
00:15:04,949 --> 00:15:11,119
...whether we plead for it or have to drug the other person,
we will read it, regardless.

1713
00:15:12,909 --> 00:15:14,749
Isn't it worse to drug someone?

1714
00:15:14,749 --> 00:15:15,749
Huh?

1715
00:15:27,499 --> 00:15:30,009
I know "The Star Traveler."

1716
00:15:30,479 --> 00:15:36,419
Mom read it for me. 
She said it's the guide for the Stars of Thistle.

1717
00:15:36,419 --> 00:15:37,769
That's right!

1718
00:15:37,769 --> 00:15:38,939
Yup.

1719
00:15:38,939 --> 00:15:42,599
My mom read it to me when I was a child, too.

1720
00:15:55,869 --> 00:15:57,839
Say, tiny Miss.

1721
00:15:57,839 --> 00:16:03,279
I can tell you're different from the other nobles,
so I wanna ask you something.

1722
00:16:03,279 --> 00:16:06,799
What kind of relationship
do you have with that blue-eyed guy?

1723
00:16:07,749 --> 00:16:09,299
What do you mean by "what kind"?

1724
00:16:09,639 --> 00:16:12,929
I mean, are you two lovers or not?

1725
00:16:16,679 --> 00:16:21,729
<i>I've been his "fiancÃ©e" for the past four years, but... </i><i></i>

1726
00:16:21,729 --> 00:16:21,819
<i>I've been his "fiancÃ©e" for the past four years, but...</i>

1727
00:16:22,739 --> 00:16:26,859
<i>I can't remember when His Highness and I first met.</i>

1728
00:16:26,859 --> 00:16:31,949
<i>I can't confidently say I've been
acting appropriately as his lover...</i>

1729
00:16:33,339 --> 00:16:35,949
"Lover" might be...

1730
00:16:35,949 --> 00:16:38,329
Why do you sound so unsure?

1731
00:16:39,759 --> 00:16:43,339
I kind of knew that guy wasn't Rene's dad.

1732
00:16:43,839 --> 00:16:47,969
But Rene has always longed for his father.

1733
00:16:48,699 --> 00:16:53,179
Please, just for today, 
let us borrow him so he can be Rene's dad.

1734
00:16:54,539 --> 00:16:58,689
Maybe you should get approval from me for that?

1735
00:16:59,049 --> 00:17:03,319
Eli, we're going to meet the chief of the Stars of Thistle.

1736
00:17:03,319 --> 00:17:05,439
The chief of the Stars of Thistle?

1737
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:08,189
There's something I want to check.

1738
00:17:08,189 --> 00:17:10,699
He's someone I don't especially want to see,
but I have no choice.

1739
00:17:13,699 --> 00:17:15,829
Mister, what're you gonna do about the mole?

1740
00:17:16,339 --> 00:17:22,329
They've interrupted my holiday.
I'll thank them plenty later.

1741
00:17:25,389 --> 00:17:26,979
Now, let's go.

1742
00:17:26,979 --> 00:17:27,909
Okay.

1743
00:17:38,639 --> 00:17:40,729
<i>How did this happen?</i>

1744
00:17:43,879 --> 00:17:48,359
<i>I was supposed to play the knight
that protects Eli today...</i>

1745
00:17:51,299 --> 00:17:53,739
D-Dad?

1746
00:17:57,959 --> 00:18:02,499
<i>When I was young, was I always making that face, too?</i>

1747
00:18:03,729 --> 00:18:06,179
Shall I tell you an old tale?

1748
00:18:06,179 --> 00:18:07,249
Yeah.

1749
00:18:07,649 --> 00:18:12,759
Once upon a time, there was a small, blue-eyed boy.

1750
00:18:13,469 --> 00:18:19,309
His mother was moved to a distant land
in order to cure her sickness.

1751
00:18:19,739 --> 00:18:27,319
The boy was so worried about what would happen
if he never saw the mother he adored so much again,

1752
00:18:27,689 --> 00:18:33,069
so every day he prayed a lot,
and wrote many, many letters.

1753
00:18:33,609 --> 00:18:38,949
Then finally, his mother overcame
the sickness, and came back.

1754
00:18:39,579 --> 00:18:43,709
However, his mother had become
a completely different person.

1755
00:18:44,559 --> 00:18:50,209
She had become a mother who was strict and cold,
and wouldn't even smile at the boy.

1756
00:18:55,279 --> 00:19:00,679
The boy was very hurt, and he may have cried a little bit.

1757
00:19:00,679 --> 00:19:07,079
Then, he realized something...
An evil wizard had surely cast a magic spell on his mother.

1758
00:19:07,079 --> 00:19:07,609
Huh?

1759
00:19:08,159 --> 00:19:12,359
From then on, he would study hard every day
because he wanted to break the spell.

1760
00:19:12,889 --> 00:19:17,029
But he didn't know how to break the spell,
and he was angry.

1761
00:19:17,029 --> 00:19:18,119
Then,

1762
00:19:19,119 --> 00:19:22,119
a princess who could break spells
appeared right in front of him.

1763
00:19:22,599 --> 00:19:28,129
The princess was a fairy of the library,
and she told him the magic words to break the spell.

1764
00:19:28,809 --> 00:19:32,629
Important things can't be seen with the eye.

1765
00:19:32,909 --> 00:19:34,609
It's "The Star Traveler"!

1766
00:19:35,309 --> 00:19:40,429
After that, the boy realized
he had lost sight of what was important to him.

1767
00:19:40,429 --> 00:19:41,889
Really?

1768
00:19:42,459 --> 00:19:45,729
In truth, the mother that he thought had changed,

1769
00:19:45,729 --> 00:19:51,149
had lovingly taken the letters from the boy
and treated them as treasures.

1770
00:19:51,809 --> 00:19:56,659
That's why from then on, even when his mother
was hard on him, the boy was fine.

1771
00:19:57,199 --> 00:20:01,159
He was extremely grateful to the princess.

1772
00:20:03,999 --> 00:20:07,919
Rene, what's importat is always invisible. Â

1773
00:20:08,709 --> 00:20:12,909
You must not lose sight of what is important to you.

1774
00:20:14,339 --> 00:20:18,589
Da... Okay, Mister.

1775
00:20:23,499 --> 00:20:23,969
Eli.

1776
00:20:25,259 --> 00:20:27,189
Here, Rene, have some if you like.

1777
00:20:31,229 --> 00:20:32,609
Thank you!

1778
00:20:32,609 --> 00:20:34,979
I'll have one as well.

1779
00:20:34,979 --> 00:20:36,699
Huh? Oh, sure...

1780
00:20:49,969 --> 00:20:53,589
M-M-My finger is not food!

1781
00:20:57,019 --> 00:21:01,469
It's not? 
But it's so sweet and delicious.

1782
00:21:01,779 --> 00:21:03,759
And there goes my appetite.

1783
00:21:03,759 --> 00:21:07,849
Tell me about it. We have to put up with this every day.

1784
00:21:08,579 --> 00:21:10,349
Mind your business.

1785
00:21:14,339 --> 00:21:17,109
I would like you to pass this on to Dr. Nikola Rezzi.

1786
00:21:17,569 --> 00:21:21,619
He'll understand if you tell him I'm here 
to pay him back for what I borrowed six years ago.

1787
00:21:22,239 --> 00:21:23,619
<i>Six years ago?</i>

1788
00:21:29,279 --> 00:21:33,129
A blond knight, you say?
I don't remember meeting anyone like that.

1789
00:21:35,599 --> 00:21:40,379
I <i>have</i> treated as many people to drinks
as there are stars in the sky, though.

1790
00:21:41,009 --> 00:21:44,389
Still the same alcohol-loving, old man Rezzi, eh?

1791
00:21:44,649 --> 00:21:48,639
Oh, are you that naive young boy from some time ago?

1792
00:21:48,639 --> 00:21:52,229
When'd you start having children in the Stars of Thistle?

1793
00:21:57,049 --> 00:21:58,739
<i>I really do...</i>

1794
00:22:03,319 --> 00:22:08,539
<i>know far too little about His Highness, perhaps...</i>

